====Noun====
{{wp}}
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable}} A method of human [[thought]] that involves thinking in a [[linear]], step-by-step manner about how a problem can be solved. Logic is the basis of many principles including the [[scientific method]].
#: {{co|en|apply '''logic'''}}
#: {{co|en|understand '''logic'''}}
#: {{co|en|use '''logic'''}}
#: {{co|en|pure '''logic'''}}
# {{lb|en|philosophy|logic}} The study of the principles and criteria of valid [[inference]] and [[demonstration]].
#: {{hyponyms|en|Thesaurus:logic}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2001|author=Mark Sainsbury|title=Logical Forms - An Introduction to Philosophical Logic, Second Edition|publisher=Blackwell Publishing|page=9
|passage=An old tradition has it that there are two branches of [[logic]]: deductive [[logic]] and inductive [[logic]]. More recently, the differences between these disciplines have become so marked that most people nowadays use "[[logic]]" to mean deductive [[logic]], reserving terms like "confirmation theory" for at least some of what used to be called inductive [[logic]]. I shall follow the more recent practice, and shall construe "philosophy of [[logic]]" as "philosophy of deductive [[logic]]".}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable|mathematics}} The mathematical study of relationships between rigorously [[define]]d concepts and of mathematical [[proof]] of [[statement]]s.
# {{lb|en|countable|mathematics}} A [[formal]] or [[informal]] [[language]] together with a [[deductive]] [[system]] or a [[model theory|model-theoretic]] [[semantics]].
# {{lb|en|countable}} Any [[system]] of thought, whether [[rigorous]] and [[productive]] or not, especially one associated with a particular person.
#: {{ux|en|It's hard to work out his system of '''logic'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|page=151|title= Sex Differences in Antisocial Behaviour: Conduct Disorder, Delinquency, and Violence in the Dunedin Longitudinal Study|date=27 September 2001|url= https://books.google.co.nz/books?id=kPdUHpwtCjYC |isbn=9780521010665|publisher=w:Cambridge University Press|author=Terrie E. Moffitt; Avshalom Caspi; Michael Rutter; Phil A. Silva|passage= This hypothesis goes by many names, including group resistence, the threshold effect, and the gender paradox. Because the hypothesis holds such wide appeal, it is worth revisiting the '''logic''' behind it. The hypothesis is built on the factual observation that fewer females than males act antisocially.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2020|author=Dan Andriacco|title=Murderers' Row
|passage="It's not a matter of opinion that she wasn't anywhere near her husband when somebody shoved a needle in him," I said, miffed. "I would have seen her."<br>"By that '''logic''', nobody did it because you didn't see anybody."}}
# {{senseid|en|Q173156}} {{lb|en|uncountable}} The part of a system (usually electronic) that performs the boolean logic operations, short for [[logic gate]]s or [[logic circuit]].
#: {{ux|en|Fred is designing the '''logic''' for the new controller.}}
# {{lb|en|countable|sociology}} A [[system]] of thought or collection of rhetoric, especially one associated with a social practice.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2023|author=Alba Rosa Boer Cueva et al.|title=Logics of empowerment in the women, peace and security agenda
|passage="We identify four logics of empowerment (political, economic, social, and security) and apply these to understanding empowerment’s historical and contemporary meanings-in-use."}}

