===Etymology===
[[File:Maelstrom-Clarke.jpg|thumb|An [[illustration]] from 1919 by {{w|Harry Clarke}} of a [[boat#Noun|boat]] [[catch#Verb|caught]] in a maelstrom ''(sense 1)'' for {{w|Edgar Allan Poe}}’s [[short story]] ''{{w|A Descent into the Maelström}}'' (1841).{{ref|group=n|name=n1|From {{cite-book|author=Edgar Allan Poe|authorlink=Edgar Allan Poe|chapter={{w|A Descent into the Maelström}}|title=[[w:Tales of Mystery & Imagination|Tales of Mystery and Imagination]]|location=London|publisher=[[w:George G. Harrap and Co.|George G. Harrap & Company]]{{nb...|Limited, 39–41 Parker St. Kingsway W.C.}}; New York, N.Y.: [[w:Brentano's|Brentano’s]]{{nb...|publishers: Fifth Avenue and Twenty-seventh Street}}|year=[1919]|section=plate between pages 96 and 97|sectionurl=https://archive.org/details/TalesOfMysteryAndImagination/page/n122/mode/1up|oclc=9270550}}.}}]]

{{root|en|ine-pro|*melh₂-|id1=grind|*srew-}}
The word was originally the name of a giant whirlpool off [[Norway]] in the [[Arctic Ocean]] which was said to destroy all ships that came close to it, likely the actual tidal pool system of {{w|Moskstraumen}} in [[Lofoten]].

It is borrowed from early modern {{bor|en|nl|maelstrom|t=whirlpool}} {{qualifier|obsolete}} (modern {{cog|nl|maalstroom}}), from {{m|nl|malen|id=grind|t=to whirl around; to grind}} (ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*melh₂-|id=grind|t=to crush, grind}}) + {{m|nl|stroom|t=stream; river; current or flow of water or other liquid}} (ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*srew-|t=to flow, stream}}),<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=n|id=112107|date=December 2020|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=n}}</ref> and was popularized by {{w|Edgar Allen Poe}}’s short story ''{{w|A Descent into the Maelström}}'' (1841).

